    The best-covering pairs above are all high-abundance genes. That
    is the dropout bias of section 6.5 visible in the output: a scarce
    antigen cannot show a large intersection on this assay whatever
    its biology, so this table ranks detectability alongside truth.

    Stated plainly, on the most favourable known pair: an AND-gate on CEACAM6+CLDN18
    reaches 4.5% of malignant cells, so 95.5% escape it. CEACAM6 alone reaches 14.4%
    and CLDN18 alone 18.5%; the safety gain costs 4.1x the coverage of the
    better single target, and an OR-gate on the same two would reach 28.4%.
    That is antigen heterogeneity measured directly, and it is the
    strongest argument in this output that AND-gating may be the
    wrong architecture for this indication even though it does
    solve the safety problem. It is a floor, so the real figure is
    better than this — but not better than the single-target one,
    which is deflated by the same assay in the same direction.

